<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n6110" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">caesūra</span></b> , ae, f.
		caedo
		<div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			<i>A cutting</i>, <i>felling</i>, <i>hewing</i>, <i>hewing off</i> ligni,
				<i><span class="en">Plin.
				16, 43, 84, § 230</span></i>; 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">silvae,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">id.
					17, 20, 34, § 151</span></i>
			— </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense2"><b>B.</b>
			<b>Meton.</b> (abstr. pro concr.), <i>that which is hewn</i> or <i>cut off</i>, <i><span class="en">Plin. 8, 26,
				40, § 96</span></i>.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b> In metre, <i>a pause in a verse</i>,
				<i>cœsura; called also</i> incisio,
					<i><span class="en">Diom. p. 496</span></i> P.; Bed. Metr. p.
			2368 ib.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
    <div class="alpheios-lex-alph-source">From <u>
            <u>A Latin Dictionary</u>
        </u> (Charlton T. Lewis, Charles Short, Oxford, Clarendon Press, 1879) </div>
